             Digital Delivers Industry's Fastest Switch
                          for IP Backbones

   ...Performance and simplicity eliminate the need for expensive
                    collapsed backbone routers...

MAYNARD, Mass., July 3, 1996 -- Digital Equipment Corporation today
introduced the industry's fastest Internet Protocol (IP) Switch, the
GIGAswitch/IP Solution.  Delivering up to 18 million packets per
second, the GIGAswitch/IP Solution combines the proven performance 
and reliability of Digital's GIGAswitch/ATM technology with the 
simplicity of IP switching to offer customers the best price/
performance solution to congestion created by backbone routers.  The 
new products are the first result of Digital's recently announced 
alliance with Ipsilon Networks. 

     The GIGAswitch/IP Solution is ideally suited for corporations 
moving to the emerging intranet backbone model, for Internet Service 
Providers, and for network managers handling Internet-related 
traffic. 

     "Networking as we know it is undergoing two fundamental shifts: 
from routed to switched networks, and from legacy LANs to intranets 
that take advantage of Internet-style technologies within the enterprise," 
said Dr. Laurence G. Walker, vice president and general manager of Digital's 
Network Product Business. "The beauty of the GIGAswitch/IP Solution is that 
it enables network managers to take advantage of both these trends by 
integrating this product set into their enterprise networks.  It really is 
as simple -- and cost-effective -- as that." 

     "The breadth of Digital's network architecture is unrivaled in 
terms of starting low and moving up to unprecedented IP switching 
performance at the high end," said John Morency, Network Consultant 
for The Registry, Inc.  "Digital's reputation of openness, worldwide 
service, and engineering excellence is further corroborated with 
this announcement."

The GIGAswitch/IP Solution includes:

     o 	GIGAswitch/IP System, which includes both the GIGAswitch/ATM 
        high-performance switch with Generic Switch Management 
        Protocol (GSMP) software, plus the GIGAswitch/IP Switch 
        Controller.  The rackmount Switch Controller incorporates 
        standard IP protocols with IP Flow Management Protocol 
        (IFMP) and GSMP software.  

     o 	IP Switch Gateway, which is a rackmount router with IFMP 
        software.  The Gateway is compatible with IP routing 
        protocols and provides access to legacy LAN environments, 
        such as Ethernet, Fast Ethernet and FDDI.

     o 	ATMworks ATM adapters with support for IP Switching on 
        WindowsNT, Digital UNIX, and SunOS operating systems.

     o 	GIGAswitch/IP Management Application, which can be accessed 
        by Netscape Navigator and similar client web browsers that 
        network managers are already using.

GIGAswitch/IP Solution Eliminates Need for Collapsed Backbone 
Routers

     By integrating the intelligence and control of familiar IP 
routing with the performance of switches, the GIGAswitch/IP Solution 
offers network managers an order of magnitude increase in 
performance over a collapsed backbone router at a much lower cost 
per port. To protect customers' investments in legacy routers, these 
routers can be moved to the edge of the network. 

     "Digital's GIGAswitch/IP solution is truly impressive," said 
Marc Josephson, President of Advanced Digital Networks, a 
Metropolitan Internet Service Provider in New York. "This solution 
now enables me to provide my customers high-speed Internet services 
entirely over a switched network, eliminating the need for high-end 
backbone routers in the core of my network." 

New Products Address Needs of Commercial Customers

     As part of Digital's enVISN network architecture, the 
GIGAswitch/ATM family has always offered features -- for example, 
redundancy, zero cell loss, and conformance to industry standards -- 
that provide the reliability and interoperability that are key to 
corporate networks. Today, Digital extended the GIGAswitch/ATM 
family with the introduction of several new options designed to make 
it even easier for commercial customers to take advantage of ATM, IP
Switching, or both concurrently.  New options include:

     o 	5-slot GIGAswitch/ATM chassis that gives customers a 
        low-cost entry into ATM and IP switching;

     o 	T1 to 622 Mb/s options that enable customers to scale 
        performance from 1.5 Mb/s to 622 Mb/s;

     o 	Modular interface connections that enable customers to meet 
        a range of media, bandwidth, and distance requirements on a 
        per-port basis; and

     o 	Available Bit Rate (ABR) service with ATM Forum rate-based 
        flow control.


Pricing and Availability

The GIGAswitch/IP Solution is available in September.  Prices begin 
at $59,000 for an entry-level package which includes:

     o 	GIGAswitch/IP System (5-slot GIGAswitch/ATM system with GSMP 
        software and the GIGAswitch/IP Switch Controller with one 
        ATM adapter)

     o 	One 4 port modular line card with multimode fiber

     o 	3 IP Switch Gateways with one 10/100 Ethernet adapter each 
        and one ATM adapter each

     o 	GIGAswitch/IP management application
  
Prices for the new members of the GIGAswitch/ATM family are:

     o 	5-slot GIGAswitch/ATM chassis begins at $5,250 with 
        availability in September;  

     o 	622 card begins at $14,950 for multimode fiber (MMF) with 
        availability in August;

     o 	4-port modular line card begins at $5,700 with availability 
        in July;

     o 	Line card interfaces (ModPHYs) start at $700 with 
        availability in July for the multimode option.  Options 
        include OC3 (155Mb/s MMF/SMF/Cat5 UTP/ScTP) as well as new 
        E1/T1, E3/T3 options; and 

     o 	Firmware V2.0 is included free of charge with the new 
        hardware; an upgrade for the existing 14-slot GIGAswitch/ATM 
        chassis is available in July 1996 free of charge through 
        Internet distribution. Firmware supporting IP Switching will 
        be available in September.

