          GSA Selects Digital for the Environmentally Safe
                  Disposal of Government Equipment

WASHINGTON, DC, July 12, 1996 -- Digital Equipment Corporation today 
announced that the General Services Administration (GSA) has awarded 
its Digital Services Division a three-year contract for the 
remarketing, recycling, and environmentally-safe disposal of 
approximately 1 million pounds of obsolete computer equipment from 
Government agencies in the Washington, D.C. area.

     As a purchasing agent and final authority for the disposal of 
Government property, GSA processes obsolete equipment in the 
millions of units per year.  Digital will provide product 
disposition and asset recovery services that will enable GSA to 
empty warehouses of idle minicomputers, mainframes, and PCs -- that 
otherwise had nowhere to go but landfills.

     Digital is partnering with GSA to offer a unique arrangement 
for the disposal of excess Government equipment. Rather than only 
pay GSA a flat scrap rate, Digital will also share a percentage of 
the proceeds from equipment that is refurbished or recycled and 
resold. 

     "This is a significant win for Digital in that it is the first 
Asset Recovery win in the U.S. Government," said Ralph Lipizzi, Vice 
President and General Manager of Digital's Multivendor Customer 
Services (MCS) Federal Government Territory. "The partnership 
exemplifies how Government and private industry can team together 
to solve some very difficult problems." 

     GSA's primary objective was to generate cash from the sale of 
the outdated equipment. However, they needed to dispose of the 
equipment in a manner that complied with EPA regulations.  Selling 
the equipment as scrap meant that the equipment could wind up in 
landfills, which is not compliant with Superfund requirements.  
Digital's solution not only indemnifies the GSA of liability for the 
disposal of hazardous materials, but also provides cash for 
computers and parts that can be resold.

     Hazardous computer materials include mercury in switches, and 
lead in CRTs and circuit boards.  The EPA estimates that in 1990, 
about 50,000 tons of lead were discarded in consumer electronics 
products -- about 22 percent of the total lead in municipal solid 
waste.  Lead can leach from landfills and remains in incinerator 
ash.  When ingested or inhaled, it can damage the brain and central 
nervous system.

     "When GSA personnel visited Digital's Resource Recovery Center 
in Contoocook, New Hampshire," commented Joe Koscielniak, Digital's 
MCS Federal Marketing Manager, "they were very impressed with how 
material is re-used, recycled, converted from waste to electric 
energy, or disposed of in an environmentally-responsible manner.  It 
was clear that Digital's ISO 9002-compliant processes offered the 
best solution for GSA."

     Digital's Contoocook facility processes 30 million pounds of 
electronic materials per year.  Less than one-half of one percent of 
that goes to landfills.  Digital has vendors and outlets for most of 
the materials received because of its extensive experience in 
manufacturing and de-manufacturing computers.

     For over eight years, Digital has been committed to the 
recycling and de-manufacturing of surplus electronic materials to 
ensure that they do not end up in landfills.

     Fortune magazine has named Digital one of the 10 top 
environmentally-responsible companies.  Other organizations that 
have recognized Digital's capabilities in this area include the 
National Property Management Association (NPMA), Rutgers University 
(De-manufacturing Initiatives), Investor's Business Daily (Nov. 6, 
1995), PC Week (Jan. 18, 1996), the Boston Globe (Dec. 28, 1995), 
and the Discovery Channel.    

