              Digital Announces Recipients of Worldwide
                   Children's Contribution Program

MAYNARD, Mass., July 17, 1996 -- Digital Equipment Corporation today 
announced the 91 recipients of its worldwide Children's Contribution 
Program, a $191,000.00 Request for Proposal (RFP) process that 
provides individual grants ranging from $500 to $5,000.  Through the 
RFP, the company invests charitable resources in local community 
organizations around the world that provide programs to children and 
youth -- primarily in the areas of education, health and human 
services.

     The Children's Contribution Program, funded through Digital's 
Corporate Contributions Program office, awarded the grants based on 
the organization's ability to provide a valuable community service 
that will have an impact on a significant number of children.  A 
sampling of the organizations in 23 countries that received grants 
includes: 

      OUTSIDE THE UNITED STATES

      o  Serve Canada Youth Service Organization (Canada)
      o  Fund for Endangered Children (Czech Republic)
      o  International Board on Books for Young People (Hungary)
      o  Chernobyl Homeopathic Fund (Ireland)
      o  Hwasung Babies Home (Korea) 
      o  The Norwegian Association for Children with Heart Disorders 
      o  Society of Friends of Abandoned Children (Slovak Republic)
      o  Young Northern Women's Development Foundation (Thailand)
      o  SOS Hotline for Women & Children Victims of Violence 	    
         (Yugoslavia)

      UNITED STATES

      o  Big Brothers/Big Sisters of Metropolitan Chicago (IL) 
      o  Big Brothers/Big Sisters of South Middlesex (MA)
      o  The Bridge for Runaway Youth, Inc. (MN)
      o  East Oakland Youth Development Center (CA)
      o  Boys' and Girls' Homes of Maryland (MD)
      o  Camp Heartland (WI)
      o  Atlanta Children's Shelter, Inc. (GA)

     According to Jane Hamel, manager of Digital's Corporate 
Contributions Program, "Our investment in these community programs 
reflects the company's interest in supporting opportunities for 
children to grow, to be healthy and to succeed.  We are gratified by 
the response we received and are pleased with the range of services 
and geographic scope represented by the recipient organizations."

     Digital's Children's Contribution Program is part of the 
company's charitable focus on investing in children and youth around 
the world.  "Digital believes that children deserve to have positive 
experiences in their lives and access to choices that will encourage 
them to reach their full potential," said Hamel.  "The company 
invests its charitable resources in programs that provide 
opportunities for children and young adults to learn, to enjoy a 
sense of well-being, and to thrive in an environment of 
