                   DIGITAL CREATES FIRST OPEN DATA
                    CENTER FOR INTERNET COMMERCE

                       
      ...Digital Internet Exchange Provides Customers Choice of
                        Telcos, NSPs/ISPs...


 ...Faster, Cheaper, More Reliable Approach for Internet Commerce...


PALO ALTO, CA, July 18, 1996 -- The Digital Internet Exchange, the 
Internet's first combined switching and commercial data center, was 
announced and opened here today by Digital Equipment Corporation.  
The exchange offers Internet service providers and their customers 
mission-critical "lights-out" operation, a full range of Internet 
services, and a choice of telecommunications carriers--a combination 
no other commercial exchange provides.

      The exchange is the first designed and built by a major 
computer maker.  For the first time, service providers can put 
business production systems at a single site and receive system 
management and administration services, according to Robert M. 
Supnik, Digital's vice president of Research and Advanced 
Development.  "Service providers now can keep their Web server farms 
and their customers' commercial data securely at a major hub of the 
Internet for instant access and transmission to multiple providers' 
network routes.  This unique concept eliminates the usual charges 
associated with reaching a network hub, and lowers costs because 
customers share services such as redundant electrical power, 7x24 
staffing, and high-speed communication links," he said.

      Located at Digital's Network Systems Laboratory here, the 
exchange supports network access for the world's largest corporate 
Internet gateway.  The lab is also home to Digital's AltaVista 
Internet search service that is accessed more than 14 million times 
daily.

      Digital's Internet exchange serves global Network Service 
Providers (NSP)s and Internet Service Providers (ISPs).  The 
exchange enables increased reliability and lower costs through 
multiple telecommunications providers.  Initial telecommunications 
carriers at the exchange are MFS Telecom and Pacific Bell.  

      ISPs can better serve their customers through easy access to 
established traffic-switching centers in Silicon Valley--the heart 
of the Internet's most explosive growth area.  NSPs/ISPs at the 
exchange can connect to MFS Telecom's MAE West network access point 
(NAP) in San Jose, CA, and Pacific Bell's NAP in San Ramon, CA, 
fully utilize Digital's dedicated switching capacity and co-location 
facilities, and still gain access to established peering 
arrangements at other NAPs.  ISPs around the world already have 
expressed interest in becoming customers. 

      Digital's exchange supports the company's strong channels 
focus.  NSPs/ISPs will be the primary channel and will have the 
opportunity to resell Digital's products and services.  Businesses 
without an ISP will be able to select one at the exchange. 

Extended Services

      The Digital Services Division has extended its Internet Server 
Management services to NSP/ISPs at the exchange.  These optional 
services include 7x24 system management, system monitoring, 
technical help desk, tuning and capacity planning, and 
administrative services.  

Digital also provides Internet Server Management services to 
customers worldwide from other production server centers. 

Advanced Technology

      "Digital's advanced GIGAswitch network switching and DEChub 
FDDI/Ethernet switches at the exchange provide state-of-the-art 
packet forwarding between connected NSP/ISP routers.  This 
technology and other unique attributes of the exchange further 
demonstrate Digital's strategy to provide an industrial-strength 
framework for commerce on the Internet," Supnik said. 

CIX, UUNET, and Best Internet Endorse Digital Internet Exchange

      The Commercial Internet Exchange (CIX), the ISP industry's 
leading trade association, is an early customer that has located its 
router at Digital's Internet exchange.  This router, called CIX 
Internet Exchange, directs commercial Internet traffic among the 
association's 150 members, who include some of the world's largest 
ISPs.  

     "Digital's Internet exchange represents a high-quality NAP 
facility that provides for a number of management services not 
traditionally offered at an exchange point," said Robert Collet, 
CIX's president.  "By co-locating a router at the Internet exchange, 
ISPs gain access to both the CIX router and a variety of services 
from Digital's 'a la carte' portfolio.  ISPs will benefit from 
reliable, high-volume capacity, and avoid costs, called back-haul 
charges, to reach a network hub."

     Richard Adams, chairman of UUNET Technologies, Inc., a leading 
provider of a comprehensive range of Internet services, said, "We 
expect Digital's Internet exchange to become a major hub for UUNET 
on the west coast.  This exchange will provide an excellent 
opportunity to meet our customers' growing needs for both 
connectivity and server co-location at the gateway of the Internet," 
he said.

     Best Internet Communications, Inc. also endorses Digital's 
Internet exchange.  The world's leading host of Web sites, Best 
receives close to five million hits per day.  Bruce Lichorowic, vice 
president of sales and marketing, said, "We have chosen Digital's 
AlphaServers as our Windows NT Web hosting platform.  Now, by 
running a high-speed T3 link into the GIGAswitch at Digital's 
Internet exchange and peering with the CIX router, we gain even 
better throughput, scalability, and backup."

HLC Internet Sees Capacity for Growth 
  
     HLC Internet, a full-service NSP in Irvine, CA, expects to gain 
"much, much higher performance from Digital's Internet exchange,"  
said President Scott Purcell.  "Digital's GIGAswitch/FDDI solution 
is next-generation technology that's ready today," he added. 

     HLC Internet will co-locate routers at the exchange. "Our 
business strategy calls for growth, and Digital's exchange provides 
the capacity we need.  We have a lot of confidence in the site. The 
team that Digital has put together is absolutely first-tier.  The 
decision of the CIX industry association to co-locate their router 
there is a big endorsement as well," he said.

Pricing

     FDDI and Ethernet port connections are priced at $5,200 and 
$1,500 per month, respectively.  Full rack prices start at $750 per 
month.  A complete price list and further information is available 
today at the Digital Internet Exchange web                
site--http://www.ix.digital.com.

     Digital Equipment Corporation is the world's leader in open 
client/server solutions from personal computing to integrated 
worldwide information systems.  Digital's Intel and Alpha platforms, 
storage, networking, software and services, together with industry-
focused solutions from business partners, help organizations compete 
and win in today's global marketplace. 
