        Digital's Prioris ZX Tops Existing Pentium Pro TPC-C
 Record for Microsoft Windows NT Server 4 0 and MS SQL Server 6 5

      Setting both new performance and price/performance records
           Digital redefines customer value in enterprise
                 computing with Microsoft Windows NT

MAYNARD, Mass., July 22, 1996 -- Digital Equipment Corporation 
today announced record-breaking Transaction Processing Council 
(TPC-C) benchmarks with its award-winning line of Prioris PC 
servers, consistently delivering industry leading solutions for 
Microsoft Windows NT Server across the enterprise.  Digital's 
Prioris ZX 6166MP/4, four-way symmetric multiprocessing Pentium Pro 
based server achieved unprecedented results of 5,740 at 
$116.93/tpmC.  This surpasses the previous record of  5676.93 at 
$135.68/tpmC set by Compaq Computer Corporation's ProLiant 5000 
system.

     "Digital is committed to providing the industry's most 
scalable line of optimized solutions for Windows NT Server, from 
the Prioris Intel line through high-end Alpha servers," said Duane 
Dickhut, vice president of Digital's PC Server Business Segment.  
"The net results of our efforts -- to provide customers with 
enterprise solutions for Windows NT Server at the best cost of 
ownership -- are clearly underscored by today's results."

     Continuing to offer customers unprecedented value in business 
critical IT environments, Digital's Prioris ZX 6000 series provides 
superior resiliency, scalability and expansion capability with 
advanced system and network management through ServerWORKS and the 
industry's premiere high-availability clustered solution for 
Windows NT Server.

     "These record results delivered by Digital's Prioris server 
and Microsoft SQL Server show not only the lowest cost of ownership 
on Windows NT Server, but also a higher level of performance 
historically available on proprietary systems only," said Jim Ewel, 
group product manager at Microsoft.  "The partnership between 
Digital and Microsoft continues to provide tangible benefits, and 
with this new evidence, customers can deploy even more demanding, 
business critical applications on a most affordable, industry 
standard hardware and software. 

     "Digital and Intel have a long-standing relationship that has 
delivered a series of award-winning Prioris server products to the 
marketplace. The latest in this series, the Prioris ZX 6000 
servers, combines Digital's system design experience with a number 
of key elements into a package that is likely to continue winning 
awards," said Dave House, senior vice president and general manager 
of Intel's Enterprise Server Group.  "Digital's innovative 
clustering and server-management technologies, combined with the 
affordability and power of the standard four-way Pentium Pro 
processor server, is compelling. It really addresses the 
availability and cost-of-ownership concerns that are so critical to 
enterprise-level customers."

Digital's Prioris 6000 Series

     The advanced symmetric multiprocessing (SMP) architecture of 
the Prioris ZX 6000 series offers one to four Intel Pentium Pro 
166MHz/512KB or 200MHz/512KB scalability, dual peer PCI buses, 
maximum expandability with up to 2.0 GB of ECC protected memory, 
1TB of total storage and 12 system expansion slots, including up to 
eight PCI slots.  This combined with industry leading PCI RAID, PCI 
Fast-Wide SCSI-2, and PCI networking options offers customers 
unprecedented system flexibility.  The Prioris ZX 6000 series also 
offers advanced system and network manageability with Digitals 
ServerWORKS, and industry leading high-availability with Digital 
Clusters for Windows NT options.
Testing Method

     The TPC-C benchmark is an industry standard, on-line 
transaction processing (OLTP) benchmark that is distributed free by 
the Transaction Processing Performance Council (TPC).  Vendors 
implement and document the tests, which are then reviewed by the 
TPC.  Once audited, the results are submitted to the TPC Council 
for publication.

